        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x,y)=(2,3)$ or $(3,2)$.\n\nSquaring gives $x+y=5$ and $xy=6$.",
          "worked": "The real square roots require $x,y\\ge0$. Squaring gives\n$$5+2\\sqrt6=x+y+2\\sqrt{xy}.$$\nLet $k=5-x-y$, which is rational. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\sqrt{xy}&=k+2\\sqrt6\\\\4xy&=k^2+24+4k\\sqrt6.\\end{align*}$$\nAll terms except possibly $4k\\sqrt6$ are rational, so $k=0$. Hence $x+y=5$ and $xy=6$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x(5-x)&=6\\\\x^2-5x+6&=0\\\\(x-2)(x-3)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(x,y)=(2,3)$ or $(3,2)$. Both give the positive sum $\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3$, whose square is $5+2\\sqrt6$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
